00:16:52,280 --> 00:16:52,780
Well, just...

202
00:16:52,780 --> 00:16:58,060
I mean, yeah, he's foolish and he lets his tongue run away with him, clearly.

203
00:16:58,280 --> 00:17:05,320
And people who've seen Dangerous Liaisons or read the book, the book by Laclau, written at exactly this period.

204
00:17:05,500 --> 00:17:06,760
I mean, this is exactly what's going on.

205
00:17:06,880 --> 00:17:12,780
It's people kind of gossiping about each other, overhearing it, reading letters that aren't meant for them, all this kind of thing.

206
00:17:12,860 --> 00:17:14,600
So it's absolutely part of the climate.

207
00:17:14,720 --> 00:17:18,760
But the Cardinal de Rohan is not smart enough, really, to profit from it.

208
00:17:19,100 --> 00:17:21,780
And so he's distraught.

209
00:17:21,780 --> 00:17:29,900
He's distraught that he feels that now that Marie Antoinette is standing in the way of him getting the kind of promotion that he feels should be his right as a Rohan.

210
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,960
So he'll do anything to try and get her back.

211
00:17:33,120 --> 00:17:40,260
And it's at this point that an impecunious woman with a sob story approaches him.

212
00:17:40,380 --> 00:17:40,540
Yeah.

213
00:17:40,620 --> 00:17:42,400
And this is a woman called Jeanne de Lamotte.

214
00:17:42,560 --> 00:17:49,780
And she claims descent from the Valois, who are the traditional royal family who kind of gone extinct in the 16th century.

215
00:17:49,780 --> 00:17:50,120
But that's obviously just...

216
00:17:50,120 --> 00:17:51,080
Replaced by the Bourbon.

217
00:17:51,080 --> 00:17:53,560
That's obviously just rubbish to appeal to the Cardinal, isn't it?

218
00:17:53,620 --> 00:17:55,040
I mean, she's a swindler.

219
00:17:55,420 --> 00:17:56,460
Well, this is the story.

220
00:17:56,580 --> 00:17:57,460
This is her story.

221
00:17:57,560 --> 00:18:03,800
And she approaches the Cardinal for some charity, which the Cardinal gives her.

222
00:18:04,260 --> 00:18:06,080
And almost certainly they have an affair.

223
00:18:07,020 --> 00:18:08,320
How does that work?

224
00:18:08,640 --> 00:18:10,120
I'd like some charity, please.

225
00:18:10,160 --> 00:18:10,720
Oh, my dear.

226
00:18:11,720 --> 00:18:12,980
I've got some charity upstairs.

227
00:18:13,140 --> 00:18:13,640
Come through.

228
00:18:14,140 --> 00:18:16,220
I think that's exactly how it works.

229
00:18:16,320 --> 00:18:16,460
Right.

230
00:18:16,560 --> 00:18:16,980
I think that's...

231
00:18:16,980 --> 00:18:17,240
Yeah.

232
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,420
So the Cardinal is a notorious womanizer.

233
00:18:21,080 --> 00:18:24,960
And the Cardinals in the Ancien Régime tend to be.

234
00:18:25,080 --> 00:18:26,580
They've got to be by law, surely.

235
00:18:27,740 --> 00:18:31,900
So having done that, Lamotte then thinks, do you know what?

236
00:18:32,040 --> 00:18:33,040
I'm going to aim higher.

237
00:18:33,240 --> 00:18:34,300
And she wants to get...

238
00:18:34,300 --> 00:18:36,840
Amazingly, she wants to get quarters in Versailles.

239
00:18:36,980 --> 00:18:41,440
And so she persuades the Cardinal to introduce her to one of the sisters of Louis XVI.

240
00:18:42,120 --> 00:18:45,860
And Lamotte has prepared for this moment very, very skillfully.

241
00:18:45,940 --> 00:18:47,920
So she's introduced to the sister of Louis XVI.

242
00:18:48,160 --> 00:18:49,280
And she faints.

243
00:18:49,480 --> 00:18:50,640
She's kind of overcome.

244
00:18:51,220 --> 00:18:54,260
And whether this is staged or whether it's real, I mean, who knows?

245
00:18:54,480 --> 00:19:01,240
But the consequence of this is that she is seen as an unfortunate who has suffered terrible things

246
00:19:01,240 --> 00:19:02,640
and therefore needs to be helped.

247
00:19:02,820 --> 00:19:09,740
And this plays into the spirit of kind of sentimentality and sensibility that is absolutely kind of current

248
00:19:09,740 --> 00:19:15,720
and associated with Marie Antoinette and her kind of vibe at Versailles.

249
00:19:16,720 --> 00:19:18,620
And so she gets these quarters.

250
00:19:18,940 --> 00:19:20,920
And she then starts using this.

251
00:19:21,080 --> 00:19:25,160
She starts telling the Cardinal that she's become great friends with the Queen herself.

252
00:19:25,680 --> 00:19:27,180
I mean, the Queen does have her...

253
00:19:27,180 --> 00:19:29,380
You referred to that in the last episode as her squad.

254
00:19:29,520 --> 00:19:32,800
So it's not that impossible.

255
00:19:33,040 --> 00:19:34,860
I mean, it could happen that she could become...

256
00:19:34,860 --> 00:19:36,280
So the Cardinal completely believes it.

257
00:19:36,660 --> 00:19:48,460
And Lamotte then starts saying, you know, the only way to get to Marie Antoinette's heart is to buy her the most notorious necklace in France.

258
00:19:48,740 --> 00:19:50,980
It had been made by the court jewelers.

259
00:19:51,080 --> 00:19:57,020
It's Burma and Bassange who had been commissioned by Louis XV to make this.

260
00:19:57,060 --> 00:20:01,800
It's called kind of slave necklace because it looks like a kind of collar that a slave might wear.

261
00:20:01,940 --> 00:20:06,340
It consists of 647 diamonds and these weigh 2,800 carats.

262
00:20:06,660 --> 00:20:09,520
So, I mean, it is massively vulgar.

263
00:20:09,680 --> 00:20:09,960
Yeah.

264
00:20:10,040 --> 00:20:13,340
And so Jonathan Beckman describes it as grotesque and almost literally unbearable.

265
00:20:13,380 --> 00:20:20,280
It more resembled an item of chain mail or something a monk might wear in penitential self-chastisement than a coveted piece of jewelry.

266
00:20:20,460 --> 00:20:20,960
It's a necklace that...

267
00:20:20,960 --> 00:20:23,040
That Donald Trump would buy for a lady of the night.

268
00:20:23,040 --> 00:20:23,160
Yeah.

269
00:20:23,700 --> 00:20:24,060
Yes.

270
00:20:24,300 --> 00:20:25,700
For a porn star, perhaps.

271
00:20:25,920 --> 00:20:27,460
Particularly if he was trying to quieten her.

272
00:20:28,640 --> 00:20:34,280
And unfortunately, the jewelers had never been able to give this to Madame du Barry because Louis XV had died.

273
00:20:34,480 --> 00:20:38,300
And so, therefore, Madame du Barry had been chucked out of Versailles and wasn't in a position to buy it.

274
00:20:38,440 --> 00:20:42,300
And so they're stuck with this enormous bill and they need to sell it to someone.

275
00:20:42,520 --> 00:20:44,340
And so the obvious person is Marie Antoinette.

276
00:20:44,440 --> 00:20:47,560
So they keep saying, you know, would your majesty, would you like to buy this?

277
00:20:47,760 --> 00:20:49,520
And it's not Marie Antoinette style at all.

278
00:20:49,580 --> 00:20:50,760
I mean, it's kind of vulgar.

279
00:20:50,960 --> 00:20:57,880
I think it's nature and simplicity and like, you know, curl, falling curls and no corsets and all that stuff.

280
00:20:58,180 --> 00:21:01,940
And on top of that, of course, it had been made for Madame du Barry.

281
00:21:02,180 --> 00:21:05,800
So it kind of, you know, it's not just vulgar.

282
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:08,440
It has associations with kind of courtesans.

283
00:21:08,540 --> 00:21:08,780
Yeah.

284
00:21:08,860 --> 00:21:09,940
So absolutely not her.

285
00:21:10,040 --> 00:21:19,580
And also she has been warned by both her mother and her husband, the king, you know, to stop displaying the bling, that it's not going down well.

286
00:21:19,580 --> 00:21:26,900
And also Monsieur Léonard, who we talked about, the hairdresser, he has been phasing jewels out from her hairstyle, going for the natural look, the flowers.

287
00:21:27,060 --> 00:21:29,180
So basically it's not her thing at all.

288
00:21:29,240 --> 00:21:30,420
She does not want it.

289
00:21:30,700 --> 00:21:36,140
So you might think, well, you know, couldn't the cardinal have done some cursory research on this?

290
00:21:36,280 --> 00:21:39,100
And he'd have realized this is a mad thing.

291
00:21:39,300 --> 00:21:41,460
Marie Antoinette would never have wanted this necklace.

292
00:21:41,580 --> 00:21:48,280
But it's at this point, Dominic, that another figure steps into the story.

293
00:21:48,520 --> 00:21:49,360
And this is someone.

294
00:21:49,360 --> 00:21:50,420
This is someone you're very keen on, isn't it?

295
00:21:50,500 --> 00:21:55,040
It's an Italian occultist called supposedly the Comte de Cagliostro.

296
00:21:55,180 --> 00:21:55,320
Yeah.

297
00:21:55,380 --> 00:21:59,140
So his story is enough to take up multiple podcasts, but just to absolutely condense it.

298
00:22:00,140 --> 00:22:02,500
He told multiple different stories.

299
00:22:02,760 --> 00:22:11,680
Some of them he said he was the son of the master of the Knights of Malta or a bigwig from Egypt or from an Ottoman pasha.

300
00:22:12,580 --> 00:22:14,780
There were stories that he was 300 years old.

301
00:22:15,020 --> 00:22:15,940
No, even longer.

302
00:22:16,060 --> 00:22:17,480
He was 3,000 years old.

303
00:22:17,600 --> 00:22:18,400
3,000 years old.

304
00:22:18,420 --> 00:22:18,580
Yeah.

305
00:22:18,580 --> 00:22:23,260
There are stories that he'd been at the wedding in Cana in the Bible with Jesus and co.

306
00:22:23,480 --> 00:22:31,800
Basically, what seems to be the truth is that in reality, either he was born in Sicily in the slums of Palermo to a Jewish family,

307
00:22:31,860 --> 00:22:34,780
or he's the son of a coachman from Naples.

308
00:22:34,860 --> 00:22:35,780
We don't know which.

309
00:22:36,140 --> 00:22:38,700
He seems to have had time as an apprentice hairdresser.

310
00:22:39,300 --> 00:22:41,240
Yeah, a lot of hairdressers behaving badly.

311
00:22:41,320 --> 00:22:43,840
And then he basically becomes a swindler, a professional charlatan.

312
00:22:43,920 --> 00:22:44,540
He goes around.

313
00:22:44,820 --> 00:22:48,560
He says he's dreamed up in a special Egyptian form of freemasonry.

314
00:22:49,040 --> 00:22:54,940
He's got a fake countess wife, and she sells the package basically to aristocratic ladies.

315
00:22:55,740 --> 00:23:01,040
And he seems to have come into contact with the cardinal in Strasbourg in 1781.

316
00:23:01,860 --> 00:23:05,900
And the cardinal, as you said, Tom, is an absolute flipping dupe.

317
00:23:06,120 --> 00:23:06,560
Yeah.

318
00:23:06,580 --> 00:23:06,920
Yeah.

319
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:09,380
I mean, that's what the whole thing about being a character from Moliere play.

320
00:23:09,500 --> 00:23:12,900
And he believes all this stuff about freemasonry on the occult.

321
00:23:12,940 --> 00:23:15,180
He believes that Cagliostro is an alchemist.

322
00:23:15,740 --> 00:23:17,760
And there are stories, aren't there?

323
00:23:17,760 --> 00:23:38,400
And I think they're probably not true, but there are stories that Cagliostro was in on the plot and held seances and occult rituals to persuade Roanne, the cardinal de Roanne, that the stuff about the necklace is true and that this is the way to Marie Antoinette's heart.

324
00:23:38,680 --> 00:23:46,100
And, of course, the thing is with always with confidence tricks is that people have to want desperately to believe that what they're being told is the case.

325
00:23:46,220 --> 00:23:47,740
And it's not just the cardinal who wants to believe.

326
00:23:47,760 --> 00:23:51,540
The jewelers do as well, because they want to recoup their investment.

327
00:23:53,860 --> 00:23:55,500
So November 1784.

328
00:23:55,660 --> 00:23:59,680
So this is a few months after the meeting in the gardens at Versailles.

329
00:23:59,820 --> 00:24:09,360
The cardinal gets a letter that has been signed, Marie Antoinette de France, which is a signature she would never use because that's not diplomatic at all.

330
00:24:09,500 --> 00:24:14,120
And the cardinal, as a former diplomat, should have known this, but he doesn't.

331
00:24:14,900 --> 00:24:17,560
And in this letter, he gets informed that.

332
00:24:17,760 --> 00:24:23,240
The cardinal is to acquire the necklace for the queen and that the queen will pay for it in four installments.

333
00:24:23,500 --> 00:24:24,840
So brilliant.

334
00:24:25,340 --> 00:24:26,520
The jewelers are delighted.

335
00:24:26,660 --> 00:24:27,740
The cardinal is delighted.

336
00:24:28,060 --> 00:24:29,180
You know, this is his way back.

337
00:24:29,580 --> 00:24:35,300
So on the 29th of January 1785, the jewelers give the cardinal the necklace.

338
00:24:35,920 --> 00:24:39,160
A courier then arrives, who claims to be from the queen.

339
00:24:39,800 --> 00:24:42,640
The cardinal gives the necklace to this courier.

340
00:24:42,720 --> 00:24:44,040
What a fool that cardinal is, Tom.

341
00:24:44,120 --> 00:24:44,440
I mean, unbelievable.

342
00:24:44,460 --> 00:24:45,580
Do you think it's the courier, Dominic?

343
00:24:45,840 --> 00:24:46,480
It's not.

344
00:24:46,580 --> 00:24:47,180
No, who is it?

345
00:24:47,360 --> 00:24:47,640
Tell me.

346
00:24:47,760 --> 00:24:48,240
Tell us who it is.

347
00:24:48,260 --> 00:24:48,800
Surprise us.

348
00:24:48,900 --> 00:24:50,560
It's one of Lamotte's lovers.

349
00:24:50,880 --> 00:24:52,080
Yeah, you astound me.

350
00:24:52,240 --> 00:24:54,560
And he goes away.

351
00:24:54,620 --> 00:24:55,960
He gives it to Lamotte's husband.

352
00:24:56,200 --> 00:25:00,080
So that's very, very marquis de Sade.

353
00:25:00,260 --> 00:25:00,860
Lots of.

354
00:25:01,960 --> 00:25:09,400
So the husband then breaks it up and he starts trying to fence the diamonds around Paris, but it's obviously dangerous to be doing it in Paris.

355
00:25:09,500 --> 00:25:12,780
So he then goes to London and starts doing it there.

356
00:25:13,940 --> 00:25:16,400
Meanwhile, of course, the jewelers are waiting for their money.

357
00:25:16,480 --> 00:25:16,740
Yeah.

358
00:25:16,800 --> 00:25:17,740
And the cardinal.

359
00:25:17,760 --> 00:25:20,480
The cardinal is waiting for signs of the queen's favor that he's been promised.

360
00:25:20,700 --> 00:25:24,440
And he goes to court and he's kind of waiting to see the queen with the necklace.

361
00:25:25,620 --> 00:25:26,300
Absolutely nothing.

362
00:25:26,540 --> 00:25:32,580
So early July, he thinks, you know, I've got to see what's going on.

363
00:25:32,660 --> 00:25:42,420
So he sends Marie Antoinette a discreet note, kind of saying, very much looking forward to seeing these, you know, you know, wink, wink, you know what I've got you, eh?

364
00:25:42,840 --> 00:25:43,480
Nudge, nudge.

365
00:25:43,620 --> 00:25:43,680
Yeah.

366
00:25:43,800 --> 00:25:45,020
And Marie Antoinette thinks he's mad.

367
00:25:45,160 --> 00:25:45,520
Yeah, of course.

368
00:25:45,520 --> 00:25:46,480
He just disposes of the letter.

369
00:25:46,560 --> 00:25:46,760
Of course.

370
00:25:46,760 --> 00:25:58,260
Early in August, the jewelers are so desperate, because they're now facing bankruptcy, that they go to one of Marie Antoinette's ladies-in-waiting and say, look, where's this money that the queen promised us?

371
00:25:58,800 --> 00:26:04,540
And they go to the queen and she, you know, she's got no idea that any of this has been going on.

372
00:26:05,120 --> 00:26:07,340
And Marie Antoinette leads the investigation.

373
00:26:07,520 --> 00:26:10,160
Personally, understandably, she's appalled.

374
00:26:10,260 --> 00:26:11,380
I mean, she's completely innocent.

375
00:26:11,520 --> 00:26:15,580
And yet her good name has been consistently abused throughout the entire affair.

376
00:26:16,760 --> 00:26:20,980
And she has this kind of dramatic declaration of purpose.

377
00:26:21,140 --> 00:26:23,200
Hideous vices must be unmasked.

378
00:26:23,280 --> 00:26:30,680
The purple of a cardinal and the title of a prince have cloaked a money grabber, a fraudster who dares to compromise the wife of his sovereign.

379
00:26:30,940 --> 00:26:33,820
France and all of Europe shall know of it.

380
00:26:34,100 --> 00:26:40,660
And so she goes to the king and the king issues orders that the cardinal be arrested.

381
00:26:40,960 --> 00:26:43,280
And so he is on the 15th of August.

382
00:26:43,340 --> 00:26:46,240
And he is arrested while he is celebrating.

383
00:26:46,900 --> 00:26:52,280
He's holding mass to celebrate the Ascension of the Virgin in the chapel at Versailles.

384
00:26:52,420 --> 00:26:57,640
He's in his full robes, you know, smoke, bells, the works.

385
00:26:57,920 --> 00:26:59,220
In come the king's guards.

386
00:26:59,380 --> 00:27:02,320
He's arrested and taken off to the Bastille.

387
00:27:02,540 --> 00:27:03,360
Crikey, Tom.

388
00:27:03,500 --> 00:27:04,500
What a story this is.

389
00:27:05,300 --> 00:27:15,200
Napoleon Bonaparte in later life said that the death of Marie Antoinette began at the trial of the diamond necklace conspirators.

390
00:27:15,340 --> 00:27:16,120
And Tom?

391
00:27:16,240 --> 00:27:17,740
That's a big claim from Napoleon.

392
00:27:17,900 --> 00:27:18,920
I mean, who am I to argue with Napoleon?

393
00:27:20,280 --> 00:27:21,300
He was there.

394
00:27:21,440 --> 00:27:30,040
Why would one say that of something that on the surface seems a little bit inconsequential and, of course, in which the queen is not really involved?

395
00:27:30,700 --> 00:27:31,100
Right.

396
00:27:31,220 --> 00:27:37,920
Because, I mean, I guess people said, well, of course, the French Revolution, a scandal about a necklace would be low down the list.

397
00:27:37,940 --> 00:27:38,860
Very low down the list, yeah.

398
00:27:39,040 --> 00:27:43,620
Bread riots, fiscal incompetence, all kinds of things, which we will be looking at in due course.

399
00:27:43,620 --> 00:27:54,340
I think that the answer to why it is so damaging to the queen is the way that the scandal comes to be teased out and the way that it goes public.

400
00:27:54,600 --> 00:27:54,960
Yeah.

401
00:27:55,080 --> 00:28:11,900
So what happens is that all the main participants in the scandal, so the cardinal, Nicole, aka the Baron d'Oliva, the Comte de Cagliostro, and, of course, de la Motte, they're all arrested.

402
00:28:11,960 --> 00:28:13,600
And they're all brought to court.

403
00:28:13,600 --> 00:28:13,920
They're all brought to trial.

404
00:28:14,260 --> 00:28:16,640
And the trials that they have are open.

405
00:28:17,600 --> 00:28:20,320
So, you know, the proceedings can be reported.

406
00:28:20,880 --> 00:28:27,980
And so this means that the queen had said all of France and Europe shall hear of this.

407
00:28:28,120 --> 00:28:29,360
This is exactly what happens.

408
00:28:29,500 --> 00:28:31,080
But it doesn't work to her advantage.

409
00:28:31,100 --> 00:28:32,420
Well, just on that, so the openness.

410
00:28:32,780 --> 00:28:40,780
I think it's important that people get a sense of what an enormous appetite for news and gossip there is in Paris in particular in the 1780s.

411
00:28:40,820 --> 00:28:43,260
So we mentioned earlier Robert Darnton's brilliant book.

412
00:28:43,600 --> 00:28:44,180
Revolutionary Temper.

413
00:28:44,480 --> 00:28:56,000
And he talks in that about how all the different lawyers, we mentioned this, didn't we, in the first half, Tom, that the different lawyers of the various participants publish these, what they call these memoirs of the case, while the case is going on.

414
00:28:56,140 --> 00:29:00,260
And these would go through multiple editions and they would sell tens of thousands of copies.

415
00:29:00,340 --> 00:29:05,280
And people would literally read them out in cafes and people would gather to read them together.

416
00:29:05,440 --> 00:29:09,840
It's a kind of collective experience of the news and of rumor and gossip.

417
00:29:10,040 --> 00:29:12,860
And this case is the perfect story for all that.

418
00:29:12,860 --> 00:29:21,260
And it actually ties together all these anxieties and sort of fantasies about Marie Antoinette that have been gathering pace for more than a decade.

419
00:29:21,440 --> 00:29:21,800
Go on, Tom.

420
00:29:21,820 --> 00:29:38,040
And I think a kind of parallel, the closest parallel I can think of from modern times would be the way in which the failure of the marriage of Prince Charles and Diana kind of exploded.

421
00:29:38,200 --> 00:29:39,660
There was gossip about it.

422
00:29:39,700 --> 00:29:40,600
There were whisperings about it.

423
00:29:40,600 --> 00:29:41,440
There was talk about it.

424
00:29:41,460 --> 00:29:42,840
And then suddenly it was all brought together.

425
00:29:42,840 --> 00:29:43,520
It was brought out into the open.

426
00:29:43,640 --> 00:29:46,260
And there was this absolute kind of firestorm of obsession.

427
00:29:46,700 --> 00:29:49,260
And I think that that is basically what happens.

428
00:29:49,400 --> 00:30:01,080
And it's devastating for Marie Antoinette and for the Crown generally because all the protagonists in various ways come to be exonerated.

429
00:30:02,560 --> 00:30:04,820
Three of them are legally exonerated.

430
00:30:04,900 --> 00:30:09,020
Four of them are kind of basically exonerated before the bar of public opinion.

431
